 maybe bouncing  back after that period of fewer uh  insolvencies  um what is the biggest challenge that  businesses are facing right now looking  forward  one challenge of course arising  um energy costs but this is mostly  hitting energy intensive well industrial  firms and indirectly other firms because  of uh depressed consumer demand but  there this is not the whole story right  we have at the same time also Rising  interest rates so uh borrowing money is  getting much more costly and this is in  particular a problem for weak firms so  refinancing is much more costly than it  used to be over the last years and it's  going to be more costly in the months to  come then we see uh increasing wages uh  in particular we saw a sharp hike in the  minimum wage uh just last week it  increased by 14  and on top of this of course we are  going to go into a recession uh with  lower demand so there is a bundle of  reasons that uh well yeah can lead to a  dangerous situation here  right now in Germany there's a  discussion about capping gas prices  essentially artificially bringing Energy  prices lower how critical could this be  for the number of insolvencies we see in  the months ahead can you make any  connection between this discussion and  what we'll see with insolvencies  it's actually hard to make a connection  between those two things because the  number of energy intensive firms going  bankrupt this  relatively low at the moment we see  quite a few bankruptcies in retail for  instance this is not an energy intensive  sector so I would expect to see not a  big influence of those well energy Cost  Cuts on the bankruptcy numbers all right  that's Stefan Mueller at the Hala  Institute for economic research thank  you so much
 

